WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.100
Key lifecycle planning covers creation, activation, use, rotation, deactivation, revocation, archival where required, and destruction.

2
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.120
Each stage should be defined before the key becomes a production dependency.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.280
Rotation is more than generating a new key.

4
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:28.980
Systems may need key identifiers, version metadata, compatibility windows, re-encryption jobs, dual-read or dual-write behavior, staged rollout, and rollback policy.

5
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.160
Without planning, rotation can become an emergency rewrite.

6
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.880
Old data and signatures need deliberate handling.

7
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.300
Encrypted records may need to remain readable while being migrated.

8
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.080
Signed artifacts may need verification against historical keys.

9
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.900
The policy should describe what remains trusted and for how long.

10
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.500
Revocation and compromise response require a clear signal.

11
00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:03.000
Services need to know which key should no longer be used, how quickly caches should refresh, and what happens to pending operations that still reference old key material.

12
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.960
C++ applications should avoid hardcoding assumptions that only one key exists.

13
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:17.620
Passing key identifiers, consulting metadata, and handling unavailable or retired keys make lifecycle operations more realistic.

14
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.180
Destruction and archival also need policy.

15
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.000
Some keys must be destroyed promptly.

16
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.180
Others must remain protected for retention or verification requirements.

17
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.720
The application should not invent that decision ad hoc.

18
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.760
The safest time to design lifecycle behavior is before production.

19
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:44.500
When key use is documented, scoped, and versioned, rotation and revocation become controlled operations instead of guesswork under pressure.

20
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.980
Lifecycle operations should also be rehearsed.

21
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:59.040
A rotation plan that has never been tested may fail because of a forgotten consumer, stale cache, missing key identifier, or deployment step that only exists in one environment.

22
00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:06.040
Good lifecycle design gives teams a predictable way to move from normal operation to replacement, containment, and recovery.

23
00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.400
Practice the workflow.
